(a) The city manager shall be authorized to suspend or revoke an occupation tax certificate or any other license previously granted by the city in accordance with the procedures set forth below. In the event the city manager seeks to suspend or revoke an occupation tax certificate, the city manager shall provide written notice to the certificate holder/licensee and such notice shall specify the violation or violations and shall be served upon the certificate holder/licensee at least five days prior to the notice of hearing. The certificate holder/licensee shall be given written notice of the time and place of the hearing.
(b) The city manager shall be authorized to suspend or revoke an occupational tax certificate in the event of any one or more of the following:
(1) The certificate holder/licensee gave false or misleading information in the original application process;
(2) The certificate holder/licensee has knowingly allowed possession, use, or sale of controlled substances on the premises, including to a minor;
(3) The certificate holder/licensee has knowingly allowed the violation of an ordinance of the city or a violation of any criminal law of the state (a misdemeanor or a felony) to occur on the premises, and which violation is materially related to the operation of said business;
(4) The certificate holder/licensee has been convicted of any drug-related, alcohol-related, or sex-related crime by the state or the city regarding an offense which was committed on the premises or which would otherwise violate the provisions of this article;
(5) The certificate holder/licensee has failed to pay any fee, occupation tax, fine, or other amount of money due to the city under this article or any other taxing ordinance of the city; or
(6) The certificate holder/licensee has altered or allowed to be altered, the business license or occupational tax certificate (license document), or the certificate holder/licensee has changed the information, defaced, destroyed, misused, abused, or improperly altered or misrepresented the business license or occupational tax certificate.
(c) In the event the city manager shall suspend or revoke any occupational tax certificate and/or business license hereunder, the suspension or revocation shall be for a period of not less than one day nor more than 365 days, within the discretion of the city manager. Any suspension or revocation shall be sent by the city by certified mail, return receipt requested. No applicant may apply for an occupational tax certificate during any period of suspension or revocation.
(d) In the event of a suspension or revocation by the city manager, the certificate holder/licensee may appeal the decision of the city manager to the zoning board of appeals for the city by filing a written notice of appeal within ten days from the date of the decision of the city manager. Thereafter, a hearing shall be scheduled before the zoning board of appeals. The certificate holder/licensee shall be authorized to continue his business operations until that date of the hearing scheduled in accordance with this section. In any hearing conducted, the zoning board of appeals shall consider, among other things, the severity of the allegations, the evidence submitted and the testimony presented, in making any decision on suspension or revocation and the duration of either. After the hearing, the zoning board of appeals may take such action as it deems appropriate, including the upholding of the action of the city manager or the imposition of such action as the city commission may deem appropriate under the facts. The decision of the zoning board of appeals shall be final. Appeals from the decision of the zoning board of appeals shall be to the superior court of Gwinnett County, to be filed within 30 days of the final action of the zoning board of appeals. In the event the certificate holder/licensee does not file an appeal from any decision of the city manager as provided herein, the decision of the city manager shall be final.
(Code 2000, § 70-232.5; Ord. of 11-3-2008)

